WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:10.110
Film Courage: What are the biggest mistakes beginning
editors make? Lawrence Jordan, Editor: So many. I I think that

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.860
you know the big was some of the biggest
mistakes that beginning editors make is

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:21.210
to be fearful to make a cut you know be
fearful to just dive in and start and

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:27.810
start experimenting and and and playing
with the material and looking at ad it

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:34.230
is something that that you can play with
and and have fun with I think a lot of

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:43.050
people are afraid to jump in and make
that cut on another level I think for

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:48.930
myself
maybe you know I I think that it was ego

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:55.469
issues kind of thinking because I had
come from these legendary editors who

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:59.760
you know had such a incredible body of
work that I thought that you know as an

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.519
editor you have to like stand by your
cut and you know and maybe not be so

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.390
flexible and stuff like that and you
know that's sort of like the antithesis

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.930
of what I believe in editor is now I
think that you have to be incredibly

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:17.700
flexible and you know I think the more
flexible you are the more you're going

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:24.780
to be able to get your ideas across so I
think I think that's sort of a a big

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.780
mistake and then you know I think that a
lot of us as editors although I don't

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.920
really consider myself an introvert I
think a lot of editors are introverted

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.030
and maybe younger editors don't
understand the importance of getting out

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:43.140
there and meeting other people and
networking and putting yourself out

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:51.030
there those are a few of kind of I think
the mistakes that people make um even

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:59.280
maybe one more would be like young
editors you know maybe not young editors

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.409
but young people starting out maybe
they've come out of film school maybe

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:08.429
they've made a few films on their own
but they're just getting into the

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:12.630
business and they're just sort of like
maybe trying to break in or

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:17.130
their foot in the door and they talk
about like their films and things like

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.700
that I think that a lot of people don't
want to hear about that kind of stuff

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.130
they they want you to do what you need
to do like go get office supplies and

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:29.970
get the lunch order correct they don't
want to hear about your screenplays at

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.860
that time you know there's a time and
place for everything of course but

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.020
that's sort of a general thing for
people just sort of breaking into

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:41.820
editing do you think the prior school of
thought was to stand by just as a writer

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.650
is going to defend the notes that
they're given and and want to do the

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.240
scene their way it was that sort of the
prior school of thought of defend your

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.410
cut I want to do it here when he comes
in the door this way and the producer

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:00.090
saying no do it after you know it was
that sort of the yeah I do think that

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:04.670
that is sort of like you know a
throwback from a from an earlier time

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:13.190
you know when the auteur theory may be
sort of you know prevailed stronger and

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:21.570
people were able to kind of or people
you know or we're able to stand by their

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.010
artistic vision I think the business has
become much more of a commercial

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:33.480
endeavor for the most part I think that
if you get involved in a film with that

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:39.240
sort of artistic vision these days that
I think you know maybe like the people

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.930
who hire you and everything they
everybody's cards are on the table so

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:51.600
it's not but again I you know as editors
we're we're service people we're there

00:03:51.600 --> 00:04:02.220
to help the director have his or her
vision you know realized so you know

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:06.300
there's plenty of times where I don't
think that maybe a scene works is good

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.190
after doing the directors notes

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:17.860
but it's it's their baby you know and
we're there to help them out I've

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:22.450
learned as I've gotten older that you
get a lot more bees with honey than

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:32.920
vinegar and there's different ways to
present your ideas other than drawing a

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:38.640
line in the sand and you know that's
usually through dialogue or conversation

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:48.280
or example maybe integrating some of the
ideas from you know your instincts into

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:54.670
theirs and maybe showing that to them as
an alternative I think you want a lot of

00:04:54.670 --> 00:05:00.610
like little battles that way and and you
know I think that a lot of directors are

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:05.830
also reasonable the good ones that when
you say you know that you know that's

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:12.220
kind of a inelegant cut was the way
Lindsey klingmann used to put it and I

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.610
think that's a tasteful way to put it
across you know it's not a bad cut it's

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:22.780
just not pretty I think that you know
good filmmakers are open to that you're

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:31.000
always gonna get people who will you
know just not want to you know hear your

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:40.150
opinion even if you're right and you
know I think that it's like a marriage

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.660
you know it's like you're not going to
be able to be married to everyone and

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:48.040
you got to find the right you know set
of people that you're gonna be able to

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.150
work with that you get along with that
you have some chemistry with it's a real

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:57.220
sort of you know marriage like
relationship because you're locked in a

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:02.530
room with that director for for months
on end so you better have some chemistry

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:07.470
together you better like each other
are you gonna be miserable

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.130
you

